          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"tolstoy-war-and-peace-688622e2fb7fe83d\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.96,\"analysis\":\"The dream figure’s past-tense «Я любил вас» (“I loved you”) presents a former loving state as true. It neither undertakes a present relational commitment nor functions as a reflex-like affective discharge; its main force is avowal/report, despite the surreal dream setting and the immediately following threat.\",\"ambiguity\":\"Dream logic makes the speaker’s identity and the precise referent of «вас» somewhat unstable, but does not significantly obscure the clause’s truth-conditional force.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"imagined\",\"description\":\"Spoken by the threatening figure of Uncle Nikolai Ilyich within little Nikolai Bolkonsky’s dream, rather than in waking conversation.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"The declaration of former love is contrasted with present obedience to Arakcheev and a threat to kill anyone who advances. This contrast frames love as a past emotional state overridden by external command. The dream’s transformations and symbolic violence complicate speaker identity and sincerity, but they do not change the core clause into a performative or expressive/reflexive utterance.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"«Я любил вас, но Аракчеев велел мне» (“I loved you, but Arakcheev ordered me”).\",\"supports\":\"The past-tense love clause directly presents a prior loving emotional state, strongly supporting T.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The clause continues: «и я убью первого, кто двинется вперед» (“and I shall kill the first person who moves forward”).\",\"supports\":\"The adversative structure contrasts former love with present commanded violence; the undertaking belongs to the threat, not to the core love clause, so it does not support P for that clause.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The narration explicitly introduces the scene as Nikolai’s terrible dream and says that terror seized him and he awoke.\",\"supports\":\"Establishes the utterance as imagined dream speech while leaving its represented semantic force intelligible.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"No description presents the words as involuntarily blurted out or as affect producing a reflex-like verbal discharge.\",\"supports\":\"Supports E=0: the ominous setting and emotional intensity do not independently establish expressive/reflexive core force.\",\"confidence\":0.94}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the avowal of a former loving state. The dream framing, coercive command, and adjacent threat are contextual complications rather than residual aspects of the love utterance’s core force.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
      ],
